Hi Process Creative team,

We sent you a client security audit for bondisands.com.au with a published gr=
ade of D (55/100). That grade was wrong. Our scoring model at the time penali=
sed every finding flatly and counted best-practice configuration items as vul=
nerabilities. Re-scoring the same scan under the corrected model gives A (90/=
100).

Three finding classes drove the wrong grade: the missing HSTS header (HTTP St=
rict Transport Security), the missing HTTP response hardening headers (CSP, X=
-Frame-Options, Referrer-Policy, Permissions-Policy, COOP) and the missing ma=
il security records (BIMI, MTA-STS, TLS-RPT).

That scan returned no critical or high finding, so the published failure did =
not describe exploit risk.

The rule now: best-practice items are reported but never scored, a scan with =
no critical or high finding cannot score below 60, and repeated findings of t=
he same class are charged at a reduced rate under a per-class cap.

Next step: reply to this note and we will re-issue the bondisands.com.au audi=
t with the corrected grade and score. No change to the client's configuration=
 is required to correct the score.

Best regards,
